  - [x] ERRNO: real semantics via the `errno-and-return` runtime helper,
        wired through open / findfile / entget / entmod / entsel /
        nentsel / ssget / ssname / read-line / write-line / handent.
  - [~] Couplings: PARTIAL — see the table below.
  - [x] Tests/gate: inventory-coverage conformance + a spec↔inventory↔
        catalogue coverage gate (`check-sysvar-spec-coverage.lisp`) exist;
        coupling tests under `autolisp-test/tests/sysvar/`.

Coupling status (verified against the built engine, not just by reading):

  IMPLEMENTED & passing:
    - ERRNO -> open / file IO / entity / selection.
    - ANGBASE/ANGDIR/AUNITS/AUPREC -> angtos / angle / getangle / getorient.
    - USERR1..5 / USERI1..5 / USERS1..5 scratch round-trip.
    - getvar/setvar unknown-name + read-only behaviour.
    - EXTNAMES -> snvalid  (FIXED 2026-06-23 in clautolisp 1.2.11; was
      previously ignoring EXTNAMES — snvalid now applies the extended
      (EXTNAMES=1) reserved-char rule, the restrictive (EXTNAMES=0) R14
      rule, and the FLAG bit that permits "|", matching the SNVALID spec
      entry's examples. coupling-extnames.lsp's relaxed-accepts assertion
      now holds.)
    - LUNITS/LUPREC/DIMZIN -> rtos default mode/precision + zero
      suppression  (FIXED 2026-06-24 in clautolisp 1.2.13 + 1.2.14):
        * 1.2.13 fixed the rtos FLOAT-FORMATTING divergences (these were
          NOT a sysvar coupling): (rtos 1.6 2 0) => "2." (want "2") and
          (rtos 100.0 1 2) => "1.00d+2" (want "1.00E+02").
          %FORMAT-DECIMAL-REAL strips the trailing point at precision 0;
          %FORMAT-SCIENTIFIC-REAL emits AutoCAD-style scientific notation
          (uppercase E, signed two-digit-padded exponent). angtos shares
          the decimal helper.
        * 1.2.14 wired the actual coupling: with MODE / PRECISION
          OMITTED, rtos now reads LUNITS (mode) and LUPREC (precision);
          the decimal result is zero-suppressed per DIMZIN (bit 3 = drop
          trailing zeros, bit 2 = drop a leading zero). AutoCAD defaults
          (LUNITS 2, LUPREC 4, DIMZIN 0) match the historic hard-coded
          values, so a host that never sets them is unchanged.
          coupling-units.lsp gained default-reading + DIMZIN deftests;
          builtins-core gained 9 regression checks.
      STILL OPEN under this heading:
        - UNITMODE: only affects engineering/architectural/fractional
          (rtos modes 3-5), which clautolisp falls back to decimal
          headlessly, so UNITMODE is currently a no-op (documented in
          builtin-rtos). Implementing modes 3-5 is the remaining work.
        - distance/getdist reading LUPREC for INPUT parsing (distance
          itself returns a real number, not a formatted string).
    - CMDECHO -> command/vl-cmdf echo policy.
    - TILEMODE/CVPORT -> vports/setview.
    - SNAPANG/ANGBASE radians-vs-display on setvar.
    - DWGNAME/DWGPREFIX -> default path resolution for file builtins.
    - DATE/CDATE/TDCREATE/TDUPDATE -> edtime.
    - MAXSORT -> acad_strlsort.
    - SRCHPATH (BricsCAD) / support path -> findfile/load.
    - TRUSTEDPATHS/SECURELOAD -> load/open/findtrustedfile trust gating.
  (None of these eight have a coupling test yet.)

Recommended next steps (by priority): (1) DONE 2026-06-24 (1.2.13) —
the rtos/distance float-format divergences ("N." for integer-valued
reals at precision 0; "d+N" vs "E+0N" scientific) are fixed in
%FORMAT-DECIMAL-REAL / %FORMAT-SCIENTIFIC-REAL; (2) DONE 2026-06-24
(1.2.14) — rtos reads LUNITS/LUPREC when mode/precision omitted and
applies DIMZIN zero-suppression (UNITMODE remains a no-op pending rtos
modes 3-5); (3) the trust-gating coupling
(TRUSTEDPATHS/SECURELOAD), which is security-relevant — NEXT; (4) the remaining
behavioural couplings with tests.

## Progress (2026-08-06) — rtos modes 3-5 + UNITMODE, and coupling tests

Branch feature-system-variables-couplings (off origin/master).

### 1. rtos modes 3-5 + UNITMODE (item 1) — DONE

builtin-rtos now formats every documented LUNITS mode; the
decimal-fallback / "UNITMODE is a no-op" note is removed.
New helpers in autolisp-builtins-core/source/api.lisp:

  - %inches->whole-fraction — splits inches into whole + a reduced
    2^LUPREC fraction, carrying a rounded-up unit.
  - %format-engineering-real  (mode 3) — feet + decimal inches:
    (rtos 17.5 3 2) => "1'-5.50\"".
  - %format-architectural-real (mode 4) — feet + fractional inches:
    (rtos 17.5 4 2) => "1'-5 1/2\"".
  - %format-fractional-real   (mode 5) — whole + fraction, no feet:
    (rtos 17.5 5 2) => "17 1/2".

LUPREC is decimal places for engineering, and the power-of-two
fraction denominator (2^LUPREC) for architectural/fractional (LUPREC 4
=> 1/16). UNITMODE is now honoured: 0 = display form (space between
whole inches and the fraction), 1 = re-readable input form (hyphen),
e.g. "1'-5-1/2\"" / "17-1/2". builtin-rtos reads UNITMODE alongside
LUNITS/LUPREC/DIMZIN. The canonical assertions are Autodesk's own rtos
reference examples for 17.5.

TODO (edge cases, headless): DIMZIN feet-inch zero-suppression bits
(0-3) for modes 3-5 (only the decimal bits 2/3 are applied, on mode 2);
engineering-mode inch rounding that lands on 12.000" does not carry a
foot (architectural/fractional do carry). Negative values that round to
zero print a leading "-".

### 2. distance / getdist reading LUPREC for input parsing (item 2) — FINDING, no code change

Investigated; there is genuinely nothing for LUPREC to do on input:

  - distance (builtin-distance, api.lisp) takes two POINT arguments
    (coordinate lists), computes the Euclidean distance, and returns a
    real. It parses no string and formats no output, so no linear-unit
    sysvar (LUNITS/LUPREC/DIMZIN/UNITMODE) couples to it.
  - getdist (host-getdist, cador/source/prompt-api.lisp) reads one line
    of text and returns a full-precision real (parse-real). LUPREC is a
    *display* precision (decimal places / fraction denominator for
    rtos/angtos OUTPUT); it does not and must not truncate parsed input.
    The spec is explicit that getdist "result is always a real, even
    when the user enters values in architectural units" — i.e. the
    return keeps full precision regardless of LUPREC.

  Conclusion: the LUPREC<->input coupling asserted in the seed list does
  not exist. The only genuine input-side enhancement would be teaching
  getdist to PARSE feet/inch architectural input ("1'-6\"") — governed
  by LUNITS/UNITMODE, not LUPREC — which is a separate headless-parsing
  enhancement, left as a TODO. LUPREC's sole coupling remains the
  rtos/angtos output path (already implemented).

### 3. Coupling tests (item 3) — DONE (breadth), one behavioural test per coupling

builtins-core FiveAM (autolisp-builtins-core/tests/builtin-tests.lisp),
all green:

  - RTOS-ENGINEERING/ARCHITECTURAL/FRACTIONAL-MODE-*,
